kubectl apply -f test-client.yaml
Step 2: Verify Baseline Connectivity
Test normal connectivity to the backend service:

kubectl exec -it test-client -n circuit-breaker-demo -- curl -s http://backend/status/200
You should see a successful HTTP 200 response.

Subtask 2.2: Generate Normal Traffic Load
Step 1: Create a Simple Load Test Script
cat <<EOF > load-test.sh
#!/bin/bash
echo "Starting normal load test..."
for i in {1..50}; do
  kubectl exec -it test-client -n circuit-breaker-demo -- curl -s -o /dev/null -w "%{http_code} " http://backend/status/200
  sleep 0.1
done
echo ""
echo "Normal load test completed"
EOF
Make the script executable and run it:

chmod +x load-test.sh
./load-test.sh
You should see mostly 200 status codes, indicating successful requests.

Subtask 2.3: Simulate Service Failures
Step 1: Introduce 500 Errors
Test the circuit breaker by generating 500 errors:

cat <<EOF > failure-test.sh
#!/bin/bash
echo "Testing circuit breaker with 500 errors..."
for i in {1..20}; do
  response=\$(kubectl exec -it test-client -n circuit-breaker-demo -- curl -s -o /dev/null -w "%{http_code}" http://backend/status/500)
  echo "Request \$i: HTTP \$response"
  sleep 0.5
done
EOF
chmod +x failure-test.sh
./failure-test.sh
Step 2: Observe Circuit Breaker Activation
After several 500 errors, you should start seeing 503 status codes, indicating the circuit breaker has opened.

Step 3: Test High Connection Load
